\title{icebergr: Read and Write Apache Iceberg Tables}
\description{
A native client for 'Apache Iceberg', the open table format used by 'Snowflake', 'Databricks', 'BigQuery', 'AWS' and 'Dremio'. R has otherwise been able to read 'Iceberg' tables only by routing through 'DuckDB' as an intermediary, which rules out writes, snapshot management and catalog integration. This package talks to 'Iceberg' directly: it connects to REST and 'AWS Glue' catalogs, lists namespaces and tables, reads table schemas and partition specifications, scans data with predicate and projection pushdown, travels back through snapshot history, and appends new data. 'Apache Arrow' is the interchange layer throughout, so scan results arrive in R without a serialisation round trip. Built on 'iceberg-rust', the Apache-governed 'Rust' implementation, via 'extendr'. Supports table spec versions 1 and 2; see the README for the full matrix of supported and unsupported features. This is a community package, not affiliated with or endorsed by The Apache Software Foundation; 'Apache', 'Apache Iceberg' and 'Iceberg' are trademarks of The Apache Software Foundation.

R has been able to read Apache Iceberg tables only by routing through DuckDB,
which rules out writes, snapshot management and catalog integration. icebergr
talks to Iceberg directly, through \code{iceberg-rust}.
}
\section{Getting started}{

Connect to a catalog with \code{\link[=icebergr_catalog]{icebergr_catalog()}}, open a table with
\code{\link[=icebergr_table]{icebergr_table()}}, and read it with \code{\link[=icebergr_scan]{icebergr_scan()}} and
\code{\link[=icebergr_collect]{icebergr_collect()}}. See \code{vignette("getting-started", package = "icebergr")}.
}

\section{What is supported}{

A deliberately narrow subset: catalog discovery, schema and partition
inspection, reads with predicate and projection pushdown, snapshot time
travel, and append-only writes. Row-level deletes, MERGE, schema evolution
and partition evolution are not supported, and several of those are absent
from \code{iceberg-rust} too. \code{\link[=icebergr_spec_support]{icebergr_spec_support()}} reports the full matrix for
your specific build.
}

\section{Credentials}{

Credentials are read from environment variables and are never accepted as
function arguments, so they cannot end up in a saved script or an \code{.Rhistory}
file. See \code{vignette("catalog-configuration", package = "icebergr")}.
}

